const e={n:"The whole Transformer",hl:["out","sm","lin","an2","ffn","an1","att","emb","tok","in"],fr:!0,bk:!1,no:"The whole figure is it",s:['<span class="k">Decoder-only Transformer</span>: the skeleton of GPT-style large models.',"Reads the text so far and computes, for every token in the dictionary, the probability that it is the next one.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Tokenize</span>: cut the text and turn it into ids</li><li><span class="k">Embedding ⊕ position</span>: each id becomes a list of numbers that also records its position</li><li><span class="k">Decoder block × N</span>: each block = masked multi-head attention + feed-forward network, each followed by an "Add + Norm"</li><li><span class="k">Linear layer</span>: gives every token in the dictionary a score</li><li><span class="k">Softmax</span>: turns the scores into probabilities</li></ul>',`<ol><li>"天空是什么颜色？" (What colour is the sky?) → tokenizing gives 8 ids</li><li>Each id becomes 16 numbers <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>In every decoder block, each character's numbers take in their context ("？" learns that the question is about colour)</li><li>Only the result at the last character "？" is used → the linear layer scores → Softmax</li><li>"蓝" (blue) has the highest probability → output "蓝"</li></ol><div class="sub">Training runs the same path, then sends the error backwards to change the parameters.</div>`,`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Before 2017 the mainstream was RNN/LSTM: one character after another, hard to parallelise; information from far-away characters fades as it is passed along.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The attention mechanism (Bahdanau et al. 2014, for machine translation) showed that letting the model look directly at the relevant words works clearly better.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Google's 2017 paper <span class="k">Attention Is All You Need</span> dropped recurrence and used only attention + feed-forward networks (the original was an encoder-decoder for translation). OpenAI's GPT (2018) kept only the decoder, built just to "guess the next token".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Any two characters are connected in one step; the whole sentence is computed at once, which suits GPUs. Later Kaplan et al. at OpenAI (2020) found that with a bigger model, more data and more compute, the error falls steadily and predictably (scaling laws).</div>`]},a={n:"Tokenization",hl:["tok"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Tokenization</span>; each piece is a token; the lookup table is the <span class="k">dictionary / vocabulary</span>; the most common algorithm is <span class="k">BPE</span> (Byte Pair Encoding).',"Cuts a piece of text into tokens and replaces each with its id in the dictionary.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Vocabulary</span>: the token ↔ id table</li><li><span class="k">Merge rules</span>: the ordered list BPE learned of "which two pieces merge first"</li><li><span class="k">Tokenizer</span>: cuts new sentences with the same rules</li><li><span class="k">Special tokens</span>: such as "end" and "padding"</li></ul>','<ol><li>Build the vocabulary: first split all text into the smallest units (characters or bytes)</li><li>Count which two neighbouring pieces appear together most often, merge them into a new token and add it</li><li>Repeat until the vocabulary is big enough (GPT-2: about 50,000)</li><li>In use: cut new sentences with the same merge order</li></ol><div>To keep it easy, this demo uses one character per token: 天=1 空=2 … 色=7 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>; in a real Chinese model a common word such as 「颜色」 (colour) may be a single token (depends on the vocabulary).</div>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>A table of whole words: too many words, and new words are unknown; single letters or bytes: sentences get very long and slow to compute.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>BPE was a data-compression method by Gage (1994): repeatedly replace the most common pair of neighbouring bytes by a new symbol. Sennrich et al. (2016) used it for neural machine translation and found a limited vocabulary can spell any word, with rare words split into common pieces.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Frequent pieces are stored whole, rare words are split into small pieces; from GPT-2 (2019) on, "byte-level BPE" can encode any text.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>It balances vocabulary size against sentence length, and no character is ever completely unknown.</div>']},s={n:"Embedding",hl:["emb"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Word vector / embedding</span>; the table that stores them is the <span class="k">embedding matrix</span>.',"Replaces each token id by a list of numbers (a vector) that expresses the meaning of the character.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Embedding matrix</span>: rows = vocabulary size, columns = d; one row per token</li><li><span class="k">Id</span>: simply the row number used for the lookup</li><li><span class="k">Dimension d</span>: how many numbers per character (this demo d=16 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>; the largest GPT-3 uses 12288)</li></ul>','<ol><li>"色" has id 7 → take row 7 → [0.12, −0.80, 0.33, …], 16 numbers <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>At the start the table is all random numbers</li><li>Backpropagation also changes this table during training: characters that appear in similar contexts slowly get similar numbers</li></ol><div class="sub">Many models (e.g. GPT-2) let the output linear layer share the same numbers as this table.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>A bare id means nothing by its size (7 and 8 do not say that "色" and "蓝" are related); a long "one-hot" list makes every two characters equally far apart, so no similarity shows.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The "distributional hypothesis" of linguistics: a word's meaning is set by the words around it (Harris 1954; Firth 1957: "You shall know a word by the company it keeps").</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Bengio et al. (2003) trained word vectors together with a neural language model. Mikolov et al.'s word2vec (2013) found the learned vectors follow computable patterns, e.g. king − man + woman ≈ queen.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Characters with similar meanings sit close together in number space, so what the model learns carries over to similar characters.</div>`]},t={n:"Positional encoding",hl:["emb"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = holds the position code",s:['<span class="k">Positional encoding</span>.',"Tells the model where each character stands in the sequence.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Position vector</span>: d numbers for each position</li><li><span class="k">How it is made</span>: the original used sine/cosine formulas; the GPT family uses a learned position table</li><li><span class="k">Addition</span>: the position vector is added to the word vector number by number</li></ul>','<ol><li>"色" is in position 7: word vector [0.12, −0.80, …] + position-7 vector [0.66, 0.75, …] = [0.78, −0.05, …] <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Sine version: <span class="formula">PE(pos,2i)=sin(pos/10000^(2i/d))</span>, odd dimensions use cos</li><li>Different dimensions turn at different speeds, like the second, minute and hour hands of a clock; together they mark a unique position</li></ol>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Attention scores every pair of characters the same way; shuffle the order and the result is identical — "dog bites man" and "man bites dog" look the same.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>The 2017 Transformer paper gives each position a vector and adds it to the word vector.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">The paper's reasons</span>With sine/cosine, the code of a position a fixed distance k away can be obtained from the original code by a linear map, which makes "relative position" easy to learn; it was also hoped to work on sentences longer than in training. The paper also tried learned position vectors with almost the same result.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>As long as every position has different numbers, the model can tell the order; GPT-1/2 use a learned table. Later improvements include rotary position embedding RoPE (Su et al. 2021).</div>`]},n={n:"Decoder block (why stack them)",hl:["an2","ffn","an1","att"],fr:!0,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Decoder block</span>, also called a Transformer layer / block.',"Lets each character's numbers take in the context and get processed once more; stacking many blocks builds up deeper meaning step by step.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Masked multi-head attention</span>: characters exchange information</li><li><span class="k">Add + Norm ①</span>: add the input back, tidy the numbers</li><li><span class="k">Feed-forward network</span>: each character is processed on its own</li><li><span class="k">Add + Norm ②</span>: add back again, tidy again</li><li><span class="k">Two residuals</span>: carry the input straight past each part</li></ul>','<ol><li>Input: 8 characters × 16 numbers <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Attention: "？" takes information from "颜" and "色"</li><li>Add the input back and normalise → the feed-forward network processes each character → add back and normalise again</li><li>The output is still 8 × 16, the same shape, so it can go straight into the next block</li></ol><div>This demo has 2 blocks; the largest GPT-2 has 48, the largest GPT-3 has 96.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>One layer does only one round of "exchange + processing"; that can hardly express complex, multi-step relations.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>In deep networks, shallow layers learn simple features and deep layers learn combinations (seen in visualisations of image networks, e.g. Zeiler and Fergus 2013). Probing studies of language models found roughly the same: lower layers lean to word form and syntax, higher layers to meaning (e.g. Tenney et al. 2019).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Repeat the same structure; each block has the same input and output shape, like building bricks; residual + normalisation make tens or hundreds of layers trainable (residuals come from He et al.'s 2015 ResNet).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Each extra layer adds one more round of "exchange + processing". Kaplan et al. (2020) found the error falls steadily as depth and width grow together.</div>`]},i={n:"Multi-head attention",hl:["att"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Multi-head attention</span>; in a decoder it is "masked multi-head self-attention".',"Lets every character look back at the earlier characters from several angles at once, find useful information and take it in.",`<ul><li><span class="k">Q/K/V linear layers per head</span>: project the vector into a small space</li><li><span class="k">Scaled dot-product attention</span>: inside each head, compute percentages and mix (with the mask)</li><li><span class="k">Concatenate</span>: join the heads' results end to end</li><li><span class="k">Output linear layer W_O</span>: mix back to the original size</li></ul>`,'<ol><li>d=16, 2 heads, 8 dimensions each <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Head 1 might learn to look at "颜" (it asks about colour), head 2 at "天空" (what is asked about)</li><li>Each head gives 8 numbers → joined into 16 → mixed by W_O → sent to "Add + Norm"</li></ol><div class="sub">What each head learns comes from training itself, not from people; some heads are hard to explain.</div>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>With a single attention, each character gets just one set of percentages, and the weighted average "flattens" different kinds of relations.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Attention Is All You Need (2017): multiple heads let the model attend to information in different "representation subspaces" at once. Comparing head counts at equal total compute, a single head was about 0.9 BLEU worse than the best setting, and too many heads also hurt; the original used 8 heads.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Split the dimensions among several heads, let each compute on its own, then join them.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>The total compute is about the same as one head, yet several relations are learned in parallel. Later visualisation studies (e.g. Clark et al. 2019) found heads that track syntax, coreference and so on.</div>']},o={n:"Q/K/V and scaled dot-product scores",hl:["att"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = every head inside attention",s:['<span class="k">Scaled dot-product attention</span>; Q = Query, K = Key, V = Value.',"Works out how much each character should take from each earlier character, then fetches their content in those proportions.",`<ul><li><span class="k">Q</span>: each character's "what am I looking for"</li><li><span class="k">K</span>: each character's "what features can I be found by"</li><li><span class="k">V</span>: each character's "what I hand over when found"</li><li><span class="k">Dot-product score</span> Q·K → <span class="k">÷√d_k</span> → <span class="k">mask</span> → <span class="k">Softmax</span> into percentages → <span class="k">weighted sum of V</span></li></ul>`,'<ol><li>"？" has q=[0.9, 0.2, …], "色" has k=[0.8, 0.1, …]; multiply pairwise and add = 3.2 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>÷√8 ≈ 2.83 → 1.13 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Do this for every earlier character → Softmax → 色 55%, 颜 20%, 天 15% … <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Output = 0.55 × v色 + 0.20 × v颜 + …</li></ol><span class="formula">Attention(Q,K,V)=softmax(QKᵀ/√d_k)·V</span>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>We need a "relatedness" score that is fast and learnable.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The idea is like retrieval: match a query against keys and fetch the values. Bahdanau et al. (2014) used an additive score; Luong et al. (2015) compared dot product and other scores.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>The 2017 Transformer chose the dot product: it can be done in one matrix multiplication, faster and with less memory (the paper's point, roughly).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why ÷√d_k</span>The paper notes that for large d_k the dot products grow (if the parts of q and k have mean 0 and variance 1, the dot product has variance d_k), pushing Softmax into a region with tiny gradients where learning stalls; dividing by √d_k brings the variance back to about 1.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why separate Q, K, V</span>"What I look for", "how I can be found" and "what I hand over" are three different things; separate projections are more flexible (the common explanation, roughly).</div>`]},l={n:"Causal mask",hl:["att"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = the mask inside attention",s:['<span class="k">Causal mask</span>, also called a look-ahead mask or lower-triangular mask.',"Makes sure every character can only see itself and the characters before it, never the ones after.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Mask table</span>: n × n, 0 on and below the diagonal, −∞ in the upper right</li><li><span class="k">Added to the score table</span>: before Softmax</li><li><span class="k">After Softmax</span>: the upper right becomes 0%</li></ul>','<ol><li>The 8 characters of "天空是什么颜色？" score every pair → an 8 × 8 table</li><li>Row 3, "是", may only see 天, 空, 是; the 5 cells after it get −∞</li><li>After Softmax those 5 cells are 0%, so mixing takes nothing from later characters</li><li>In training the whole sentence "天空是什么颜色？蓝" goes in at once and every position practises "guess the next character": position 1 guesses "空", position 2 guesses "是" … position 8, "？", guesses "蓝" (this demo only scores the answer part)</li></ol>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>In training the whole sentence goes in at once; if "？" could see the "蓝" after it, it would be peeking at the answer and learn nothing; and in real use the later characters are not written yet — training and use must match.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Earlier RNN language models worked one character after another and naturally saw only the past; a Transformer processes the whole sentence in parallel, so it needs another way to block the future.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>The 2017 Transformer paper sets the scores of "later positions" to −∞ in the decoder's self-attention, keeping it "autoregressive".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>A language model's probability splits by the chain rule: <span class="formula">P(x1)·P(x2|x1)·P(x3|x1,x2)…</span>; the mask makes each position depend only on what came before, and the whole sentence still trains in parallel instead of waiting character by character like an RNN.</div>`]},r={n:"Residual + layer normalisation",hl:["an1","an2"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Residual connection</span> ("Add") + <span class="k">Layer Normalization</span> ("Norm"), together called Add & Norm.',"Residual: add the input unchanged to the output so nothing is lost; normalisation: bring the numbers back to a common size so training is stable.",`<ul><li><span class="k">Residual bypass</span>: the input skips the sub-layer and goes straight to the plus</li><li><span class="k">Addition</span>: output = input + sublayer(input)</li><li><span class="k">Layer norm</span>: take the mean and variance of one character's d numbers and make them mean 0, variance 1</li><li><span class="k">γ and β</span>: learned scale and shift</li></ul>`,'<ol><li>Input x = [2, 4, 6], the sub-layer gives [1, 0, −1] <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Add → [3, 4, 5]</li><li>Mean 4, standard deviation ≈ 0.82 → normalised [−1.22, 0, 1.22]</li><li>Then × γ + β</li></ol><div class="sub">The original puts the normalisation after the sub-layer (as drawn); from GPT-2 on it usually goes before the sub-layer (Pre-LN), which is more stable when very deep.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Past a certain depth even the training error gets worse ("degradation", not overfitting); the size of each layer's numbers keeps drifting and training is unstable.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>He et al. (2015, Microsoft Research Asia, ResNet) found a plain 56-layer network did worse than a 20-layer one.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Let each layer learn only "how much to change the input" and add the input back; they trained a 152-layer network. Normalisation: Ba, Kiros and Hinton proposed layer norm in 2016; it does not depend on batch size and suits sequence models (its forerunner was 2015's batch norm).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>The addition lets gradients flow straight back along the bypass to earlier layers without vanishing; normalisation keeps each layer's input at a stable scale, so training is faster and steadier. Xiong et al. (2020) analysed why Pre-LN is more stable.</div>`]},p={n:"Feed-forward network",hl:["ffn"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Feed-forward network</span> (FFN), also called an MLP (multi-layer perceptron).',"Processes each character's numbers on its own with a non-linear step, refining what attention brought in.",'<ul><li><span class="k">First linear layer</span>: widen d → 4d (this demo 16 → 64 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>)</li><li><span class="k">Activation</span>: the original uses ReLU, the GPT family GELU</li><li><span class="k">Second linear layer</span>: narrow 4d → d</li><li><span class="k">One set of parameters for every position</span>: characters do not exchange here</li></ul>','<ol><li>The 16 numbers of "色" × W1 + b1 → 64 numbers</li><li>Activation: ReLU turns negatives into 0 (GELU is a smoother version)</li><li>× W2 + b2 → 16 numbers</li><li>Sent to "Add + Norm"</li></ol>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>The output of attention is basically a weighted average (linear combination) of V; on its own it limits what the model can express — non-linearity is needed.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Universal approximation theorem (Cybenko 1989; Hornik et al. 1989): a two-layer network with a non-linear activation can approximate any continuous function if wide enough.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>The 2017 Transformer puts an FFN after every attention: 512 → 2048 → 512 in the original (4× is an empirical choice). GELU was proposed by Hendrycks and Gimpel in 2016.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Attention "exchanges", the FFN "digests". Geva et al. (2021) found FFNs act like "key-value memories" holding much knowledge learned from the training data.</div>']},d={n:"Linear layer (output head)",hl:["lin"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Linear layer / output head</span>, the LM head (also called unembedding: turns a vector back into scores over the vocabulary).',"Gives every token in the dictionary a score (a logit); the higher the score, the likelier it is the next character.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Weight matrix W</span>: d × vocabulary size (this demo 16 × 14 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>)</li><li><span class="k">Output logits</span>: one score per token</li><li><span class="k">Final norm</span>: Pre-LN models (e.g. GPT-2) add one more LayerNorm in front of it</li><li><span class="k">Weight sharing</span>: often shares its numbers with the embedding table</li></ul>','<ol><li>Take only the 16 numbers h at the last position, "？"</li><li>Dot h with every column of W → 蓝 4.1, 绿 1.3, 红 1.0, 天 −0.5 … <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Send to Softmax to get probabilities</li></ol><div class="sub">In training every position is scored: every position guesses its own next character.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>A decoder block outputs the "meaning" as d numbers; that has to become "how likely each character is".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The most natural score is similarity: Press and Wolf (2017) and Inan et al. (2017) also found that sharing the input embedding table with the output table works as well or better with fewer parameters; the 2017 Transformer shares them too.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Use the simplest linear map: dot h with every character's "output vector"; the more alike, the higher the score.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>A linear layer is simple and differentiable, and its scores pass through Softmax to become probabilities; shared weights let "reading in" and "writing out" use the same meanings.</div>`]},c={n:"Softmax and probability",hl:["sm","out"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"",s:['<span class="k">Softmax</span> (normalised exponential function).',"Turns a set of scores of any size into probabilities that are all positive and add up to 100%.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Exponential e^x</span>: makes everything positive and widens the gaps</li><li><span class="k">Sum</span>: add up all the exponentials</li><li><span class="k">Divide</span>: divide each by the sum</li><li><span class="k">Implementation trick</span>: subtract the maximum first to avoid overflow</li></ul>','<ol><li>Scores (three characters only): 蓝 4.1, 绿 1.3, 红 1.0 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>e^x ≈ 60.3, 3.7, 2.7, sum ≈ 66.7</li><li>Probabilities ≈ 蓝 90%, 绿 5.5%, 红 4.1%</li></ol><span class="formula">pᵢ = e^zᵢ ÷ (e^z₁ + e^z₂ + …)</span>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>The linear layer's scores can be positive or negative with no fixed total, so they cannot be used as probabilities directly.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The form is the same as the Boltzmann distribution of statistical physics; the name "softmax" was given by Bridle in 1990 for neural-network classification.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Use the exponential: always positive, keeps the order, differentiable everywhere; it is the natural extension of logistic regression to many classes.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Together with cross-entropy the gradient is very simple: <span class="formula">probability − correct answer</span>. Dividing the scores by a temperature T first tunes how "sure" the output is (see K15).</div>`]},h={n:"Loss function: cross-entropy",hl:["out"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = the output probabilities",s:['<span class="k">Cross-entropy loss</span>; in language models also called negative log-likelihood (NLL).','One number that measures "how low the model put the right answer"; training means pushing it down.','<ul><li><span class="k">Model probability p</span>: the Softmax output</li><li><span class="k">Correct answer</span>: only "蓝" is 1, all others 0 (one-hot)</li><li><span class="k">Take the right answer’s probability</span> p(蓝)</li><li><span class="k">Negative log</span>: loss = −ln p(蓝)</li><li><span class="k">Average</span>: over all positions and all examples</li></ul>','<ol><li>At the start p(蓝) = 12% → loss = −ln 0.12 ≈ 2.12 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>After training p(蓝) = 95% → loss ≈ 0.05 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Right and confident → near 0; giving the right answer only 1% → about 4.6</li></ol>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>We need one number that says "how wrong" and can be differentiated to tell every parameter which way to move.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Shannon's 1948 information theory: an event of probability p carries −log p of information; cross-entropy measures "how much extra information it takes to encode the real data with the model's distribution".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Minimising cross-entropy = making the training data as probable as possible (maximum-likelihood estimation, set out systematically by Fisher in the early 20th century).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>With Softmax the gradient = probability − correct answer, simple and stable; the wilder the guess, the bigger the gradient and the faster the fix (better suited to classification than squared error). Kaplan et al. (2020) found cross-entropy falls as a power law as model, data and compute grow.</div>`]},m={n:"Backpropagation and parameter update",hl:["out","sm","lin","an2","ffn","an1","att","emb","tok","in"],fr:!0,bk:!0,no:"Orange = error passed down from the top",s:['<span class="k">Backpropagation</span> + <span class="k">gradient descent</span>; the usual optimiser is <span class="k">Adam</span>.','Works out how much each parameter is "to blame" for the error (its gradient), then moves every parameter a small step in the direction that lowers the error.','<ul><li><span class="k">Forward</span>: compute the prediction and the loss, keep the intermediate results</li><li><span class="k">Backward</span>: start from the loss and go back layer by layer with the chain rule</li><li><span class="k">Gradient</span>: one number per parameter — how much the loss changes when it grows a little</li><li><span class="k">Update</span>: new parameter = old parameter − learning rate × gradient</li><li><span class="k">Adam</span>: tunes the step size of each parameter automatically</li></ul>','<ol><li>At the top: gradient of 蓝 = 0.12 − 1 = −0.88 (raise its score); 红 = 0.11 − 0 = +0.11 (lower it) <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Passed down layer by layer to the linear layer, each decoder block and the embedding table</li><li>A parameter w = 0.50 with gradient 0.20 and learning rate 0.1 → new w = 0.48 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Repeat many times (200 steps in this demo) and the error slowly falls</li></ol>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>There are thousands of parameters (hundreds of billions in big models); trying them one by one is far too slow.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The chain rule of calculus: the derivative of nested functions is the product of each layer's derivative; going from back to front gives all the gradients in one pass, at about the cost of one forward pass.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Reverse-mode differentiation goes back to Linnainmaa (1970) and Werbos (1974); the 1986 Nature paper by Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams made it widely known for neural networks.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Stepping against the gradient lowers the error fastest (steepest descent, Cauchy 1847). Adam (Kingma and Ba 2014) combines "momentum" with "each parameter's own step size" and is a common choice for training Transformers.</div>`]},u={n:"Generation loop, sampling and temperature",hl:["sm","out","in"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"The output is fed back into the input",s:['<span class="k">Autoregressive generation</span>, <span class="k">sampling</span>, <span class="k">temperature</span>.',"Produces one character at a time, appends it and guesses the next, until done; temperature sets whether the answer is safe or varied.",`<ul><li><span class="k">One forward pass</span>: gives the next character's probabilities</li><li><span class="k">Picking rule</span>: greedy (always the top), draw by probability, top-k / top-p (draw only among the leading candidates)</li><li><span class="k">Temperature T</span>: divide the scores by T before Softmax</li><li><span class="k">Append</span>: the chosen character is added after the input</li><li><span class="k">Stop</span>: at the end token or the length limit</li><li><span class="k">KV cache</span>: keep the K and V of earlier characters instead of recomputing</li></ul>`,'<ol><li>"天空是什么颜色？" → out comes "蓝" → "…？蓝" → guess again → until the end token</li><li>Temperature: scores 4.1 / 1.3 / 1.0 <span class="ex">example numbers</span><br>T=1 → 90% / 5.5% / 4.1%<br>T=0.5 → 99.4% / 0.4% / 0.2%<br>T=2 → 69% / 17% / 15%</li></ol>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>The model can only "guess the next character", but we want it to write a whole passage.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The chain rule splits the probability of a sentence into a product per character:<br><span class="formula">P(sentence)=P(c1)·P(c2|c1)·…</span><br>so generating character by character writes sentences from the distribution the model learned, matching the causal mask used in training.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Holtzman et al. (2019) found that always taking the top choice or chasing maximum probability makes text repetitive and dull; they proposed top-p (nucleus) sampling.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Temperature borrows a concept from statistical physics: small T → a sharper, surer distribution; large T → flatter and more varied. Tune between "steady" and "fresh" as needed.</div>']},g={n:"Vectors",hl:["emb","att","ffn","lin"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Vector</span>: a few numbers in a row; the count of numbers is its <span class="k">dimension</span>.',`Uses a list of numbers to stand for a character's meaning, so that "meaning" can be computed with.`,'<ul><li><span class="k">Component</span>: each number in the list, one per direction</li><li><span class="k">Dimension d</span>: how many numbers (on this site d=16 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>)</li><li><span class="k">Length</span>: <span class="formula">|v| = √(sum of squared components)</span></li><li><span class="k">Direction</span>: where the arrow points, i.e. which way the meaning leans</li></ul>',`<ol><li>In 2-D it can be drawn: "色" = [3, 2] is the arrow 3 across and 2 up from the origin <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Length = √(3²+2²) ≈ 3.61</li><li>Adding two vectors = adding component by component: [3, 2] + [1, 0.5] = [4, 2.5]</li><li>With more dimensions the rules are the same, it just cannot be drawn</li></ol><div class="sub">Word vectors, Q/K/V and every layer's intermediate results are all vectors.</div>`,'<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Computers only compute with numbers; to let them "compare meanings", meanings must first be written as numbers whose distances can be compared.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The mathematics of vectors took shape roughly in the 19th century: Hamilton (1843, quaternions) and Grassmann (1844) introduced many-dimensional quantities; at the end of the century Gibbs and Heaviside set out the vector notation used today (roughly).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Give every character a d-dimensional vector, random at first and slowly adjusted in training (see card K03, Embedding).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>A list of numbers can show "which way it leans" (direction) and compare "how alike" (angle, distance); with many dimensions, many kinds of features can be kept at once.</div>']},b={n:"Dot product and cosine similarity",hl:["att","lin"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Dot product</span> (also inner product); divided by the two lengths it is the <span class="k">cosine similarity</span>.','One number that measures how "aligned" two vectors are: attention scores every pair of characters with it.','<ul><li><span class="k">Multiply position by position, then add</span>: <span class="formula">q·k = q₁k₁ + q₂k₂ + …</span></li><li><span class="k">Geometric meaning</span>: <span class="formula">q·k = |q|·|k|·cosθ</span></li><li><span class="k">Cosine</span>: <span class="formula">cosθ = q·k ÷ (|q|·|k|)</span>, between −1 and 1</li></ul>','<ol><li>q = [3, 1], k = [2, 2.5] <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Position by position: 3×2 + 1×2.5 = 8.5</li><li>Geometric: 3.16 × 3.20 × 0.84 ≈ 8.5 (both ways agree)</li><li>Angle about 33°, cos ≈ 0.84: fairly aligned</li><li>Same direction positive, perpendicular 0, opposite negative</li></ol><div class="sub">Example: 0.8×0.5 + 0.2×(−0.1) + 0.4×0.9 = 0.74 <span class="ex">example numbers</span>; the X3 cell multiplies 8 pairs of numbers and adds them.</div>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>To compare "how alike in meaning" two vectors are we need a score that is fast and differentiable.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>In information retrieval, Salton et al. around the 1970s used the "vector space model" and compared documents by cosine similarity (roughly). In machine translation, Luong et al. (2015) compared several attention scores; the dot product is the simplest.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Transformer attention (2017) scores directly with q·k, then divides by √d (see KM12).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Only multiplications and additions; the dot products of all pairs form one matrix multiplication (see KM03), which GPUs compute extremely fast; training pushes q and k that "should relate" toward the same direction.</div>']},y={n:"Matrices and matrix multiplication",hl:["att","ffn","lin"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Matrix</span>: numbers arranged in a "rows × columns" table; <span class="k">matrix multiplication</span>.',"Transforms and scores the vectors of all characters at once; most of a model's parameters are matrices.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Shape</span>: 3 rows × 2 columns is written 3×2</li><li><span class="k">Multiplication rule</span>: columns on the left = rows on the right</li><li><span class="k">Each cell</span>: one row on the left · one column on the right (one dot product)</li><li><span class="k">Transpose</span> Kᵀ: rows become columns and columns rows</li></ul>','<ol><li>X (3 characters × 3 dims) × W_Q (3×2) = Q (3×2) <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>The "色" row [−0.6, 0.8, 0.2] · column 1 of W_Q [0.5, 0.2, −0.4]</li><li>= −0.30 + 0.16 − 0.08 = −0.22</li><li>Then S = Q × Kᵀ: "色 asks 空" = (−0.22)×(−0.31) + 0.94×0.18 ≈ 0.24</li><li>One multiplication gives the score table of all pairs</li></ol><div class="sub">Linear layers, feed-forward networks and Q/K/V are all computed this way.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>A sentence has many characters and each has many dimensions; computing one by one is slow and hard to write as one formula.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>A matrix writes "a batch of linear operations" as one table; Cayley's 1858 paper set out systematic rules for adding and multiplying matrices.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Stack all characters' vectors into a matrix X and store the parameters as matrices too (W_Q, W_K, W_V, linear layers); every step is a matrix multiplication.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>The cells of a matrix product do not depend on each other and can be computed at once; GPUs are built for exactly this kind of mass multiply-add, which is why big models are computable.</div>`]},f={n:"softmax",hl:["att","sm"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">softmax</span>: turns a list of scores into probabilities that add up to 100%.',`In attention it turns scores into "how much to attend"; at the output it turns every dictionary character's score into a probability.`,'<ul><li><span class="k">Raw scores</span> (logits): positive or negative</li><li><span class="k">Exponential</span> eˣ: makes all positive (see KM05)</li><li><span class="k">Normalise</span>: divide each by the sum</li></ul>','<ol><li>Scores 蓝 2.0, 绿 1.0, 红 0.1, 天 −0.5 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Take eˣ: 7.39, 2.72, 1.11, 0.61, sum 11.82</li><li>Divide by the sum: 62.5%, 23.0%, 9.3%, 5.1%</li><li>Formula: <span class="formula">softmax(xᵢ) = exp(xᵢ) ÷ Σⱼ exp(xⱼ)</span></li></ol><div class="sub">Subtracting the same number from all scores changes nothing; programs usually subtract the maximum first so eˣ does not overflow.</div>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Scores can be positive or negative and do not add to 1, so they are not probabilities; they must also be differentiable to train.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The same form appeared long before as the Boltzmann distribution of statistical physics; using it in neural networks under the name softmax goes back roughly to Bridle around 1990.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>First eˣ to make positive, then divide by the sum; higher scores get more, but the others are never pushed to exactly 0.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Keeps the order, differentiable everywhere; paired with cross-entropy the gradient is exactly "probability − correct answer" (see KM07), very simple.</div>']},v={n:"Exponentials and logarithms",hl:["sm","out"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Exponential</span> eˣ and <span class="k">natural logarithm</span> ln; e ≈ 2.718, the two undo each other.','eˣ lets softmax make scores positive; ln lets cross-entropy turn "multiply a chain of probabilities" into "add a list of numbers".','<ul><li><span class="k">eˣ</span>: always above 0; every +1 in x multiplies the result by 2.718</li><li><span class="k">ln</span>: <span class="formula">ln(eˣ) = x</span>; ln 1 = 0, the smaller the probability the more negative its ln</li><li><span class="k">Key property</span>: <span class="formula">ln(a×b) = ln a + ln b</span></li></ul>',`<ol><li>e² ≈ 7.39, ln 7.39 ≈ 2 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Three examples guessed right with probability 0.12, 0.10, 0.11; their product = 0.00132, too small to read</li><li>Take ln and add: −(ln 0.12 + ln 0.10 + ln 0.11) ≈ 6.63, easy to read and compute</li><li>A product over thousands of examples gets too small for the computer (underflow); adding lns has no such problem</li></ol><div class="sub">The error in layer 4's TR4 is computed exactly this way: −ln p(蓝).</div>`,'<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>Probabilities have to be multiplied and keep shrinking; a product is also hard to take apart when differentiating.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Logarithms were first published by Napier in 1614, precisely to turn heavy multiplications into additions; exponentials and logarithms to base e were studied systematically in the 18th century by Euler and others (roughly).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>softmax uses eˣ; the loss uses −ln probability; the product over many examples becomes a sum of errors, then an average.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>eˣ is always positive, keeps the order and is its own derivative; ln turns multiplication into addition, is numerically stable, and after differentiating the terms do not interfere.</div>']},k={n:"Probability and maximum likelihood",hl:["sm","out"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Probability</span>: between 0 and 100%, all adding up to 100%; <span class="k">maximum likelihood</span>: choose the parameters that make the real answers as probable as possible.','Sets a goal for "how well the model guesses": the real next characters in the training data should get probabilities as high as possible.',`<ul><li><span class="k">Probability distribution</span>: one probability per dictionary character</li><li><span class="k">Likelihood</span>: the product of the real answers' probabilities over all examples</li><li><span class="k">Log-likelihood</span>: after ln it becomes a sum (see KM05)</li></ul>`,'<ol><li>Model A: the real answers of three examples get 0.12, 0.10, 0.11 → likelihood 0.00132 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Model B: 0.62, 0.70, 0.80 → likelihood 0.3472, far larger</li><li>Add the −lns: A ≈ 6.63, B ≈ 1.06</li><li>Larger likelihood ⇔ smaller sum of −ln, so "maximum likelihood" is "minimise cross-entropy"</li></ol><div class="sub">Training makes this number smaller and smaller.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>"Is the model good?" needs a standard that can be computed, compared and optimised.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The statistician Fisher proposed and named maximum likelihood in the early 20th century (roughly 1912–1922): good parameters should make the observed data most "likely to happen".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>A language model treats the "next character" at every position as an example and maximises the product of the real answers' probabilities (a sum after ln).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>A clear, differentiable goal; more data makes the estimate more reliable; it fits softmax + cross-entropy exactly.</div>`]},w={n:"Cross-entropy loss",hl:["out"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Cross-entropy loss</span>; the "loss" on a training curve is usually this.',"One number saying how wrong this guess was; training makes it smaller.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Correct answer</span>: just one character is 100%</li><li><span class="k">Model probability</span>: the softmax output</li><li><span class="k">Formula</span>: <span class="formula">L = −ln p(correct answer)</span></li></ul>','<ol><li>Say "蓝" gets only 12% → L = −ln 0.12 ≈ 2.12 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>After some learning 62% → 0.48; 95% → 0.05; 1% → 4.61</li><li>Gradient = probability − correct answer: probabilities [0.62, 0.23, 0.09, 0.05] with the correct answer in cell 1, "蓝" → [−0.38, 0.23, 0.09, 0.05]</li><li>The negative cell (蓝) should rise, the others fall</li></ol><div class="sub">Averaged over many examples, this is the loss of one training step.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>We need an error that is near 0 for good guesses, large for wrong ones, and simple to differentiate.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Shannon's 1948 information theory defined "entropy"; Kullback and Leibler (1951) gave a measure of how two distributions differ (KL divergence). Cross-entropy = entropy + KL; with fixed correct answers, minimising cross-entropy minimises KL, which is maximum likelihood (see KM06).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Take only the −ln probability of the correct cell; differentiate it together with softmax.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Being confident and wrong is punished hard; the gradient is just "probability − correct answer", simple and unlikely to vanish.</div>`]},x={n:"Derivatives and gradients",hl:["out","sm","lin","an2","ffn","an1","att","emb","tok","in"],fr:!1,bk:!0,no:"Orange = blocks computed backwards in training",s:['<span class="k">Derivative</span>: when one quantity changes a little, how many times as much another changes; the derivatives of many parameters in a row are the <span class="k">gradient</span>.',"Tells every parameter which way and how much to change so the error goes down.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Slope</span>: the steepness of the error curve at the current point</li><li><span class="k">Sign</span>: negative = moving right (larger) lowers the error; positive = move left</li><li><span class="k">Gradient</span>: one derivative per parameter, possibly hundreds of millions</li></ul>',`<ol><li>Example: error <span class="formula">L(w) = (w − 2)² + 0.5</span> <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Derivative <span class="formula">L'(w) = 2(w − 2)</span>, at w = 0 it is −4</li><li>Check: (L(0.001) − L(0)) ÷ 0.001 ≈ −3.999</li><li>Negative slope → make w larger and the error falls</li><li>At the bottom, w = 2, the slope is 0</li></ol><div class="sub">The gradient points "uphill fastest", so training walks the opposite way (KM10).</div>`,'<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>There are too many parameters to nudge each one and look at the error; we must compute "which way to change" directly.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Derivatives come from the calculus developed independently by Newton and Leibniz in the 17th century; with several variables, the partial derivatives in every direction together form the gradient.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Make every step of the model differentiable and compute the gradient of the error with respect to all parameters in training (how to do it efficiently: KM09).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>In a tiny range a curve is almost straight, so the slope shows the downhill direction accurately; each step needs only local information.</div>']},T={n:"Chain rule and backpropagation",hl:["out","sm","lin","an2","ffn","an1","att","emb","tok","in"],fr:!1,bk:!0,no:"Orange = blocks computed backwards in training",s:[`<span class="k">Chain rule</span>: for nested functions, the total derivative = the product of each layer's derivative; applying it from back to front in bulk is <span class="k">backpropagation</span>.`,"One backward pass computes the gradients of all parameters.",`<ul><li><span class="k">Local derivative</span>: each layer's own "magnification"</li><li><span class="k">Forward cache</span>: intermediate results saved on the way up</li><li><span class="k">Backward order</span>: start from the error and multiply layer by layer toward the front</li></ul>`,`<ol><li>Three layers: ×2 → ×3 → ×0.5 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>x from 1 to 1.1 moves the result from 3 to 3.3: magnified 3 times</li><li>Chain rule: <span class="formula">dL/dx = 0.5 × 3 × 2 = 3</span></li><li>Backward: start at 1, ×0.5 → 0.5, ×3 → 1.5, ×2 → 3</li><li>Each layer passes its result to the layer before, nothing is recomputed</li></ol><div class="sub">In a real network each layer's "multiplier" is a matrix; the idea is the same.</div>`,`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>A deep network has many layers and a huge number of parameters; differentiating each parameter separately would be impossibly expensive.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>The chain rule goes back to Leibniz's 17th-century calculus (roughly). Linnainmaa (1970) introduced the "reverse mode" of automatic differentiation; Werbos's 1974 PhD thesis proposed using it to train neural networks.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams's 1986 Nature paper trained multi-layer networks with backpropagation and made the method widely known.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Intermediate results are reused layer by layer; all gradients cost only about one to two times more than one forward pass.</div>`]},K={n:"Gradient descent and learning rate",hl:["out","sm","lin","an2","ffn","an1","att","emb","tok","in"],fr:!1,bk:!0,no:"Orange = blocks computed backwards in training",s:['<span class="k">Gradient descent</span>: change the parameters step by step against the gradient; how far each step goes is set by the <span class="k">learning rate</span>.','Moves the parameters step by step to where the error is low — that is "training".','<ul><li><span class="k">Update rule</span>: <span class="formula">new w = old w − learning rate × slope</span></li><li><span class="k">Learning rate η</span>: the step size</li><li><span class="k">Mini-batch</span>: each step estimates the gradient from a small batch of examples (stochastic gradient descent, SGD)</li></ul>','<ol><li>L(w) = (w − 2)² + 0.5, starting from w = 0 <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>η = 0.3: w = 0.00, 1.20, 1.68, 1.87, 1.95…, error 4.5 → 0.50</li><li>η = 1.1 is too big: w = 0.00, 4.40, −0.88, 5.46, −2.15, jumping further and further</li><li>η = 0.05 is too small: after 4 steps w has only reached 0.69</li></ol><div class="sub">Real training usually uses improved versions such as Adam and lets the learning rate rise first, then fall.</div>',`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>With the gradient in hand we still have to decide how to update: too big a step jumps over the valley, too small is too slow.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Cauchy proposed gradient descent in 1847; Robbins and Monro's 1951 stochastic approximation laid the ground for "approaching step by step with noisy estimates".</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Each step takes a small batch, computes the gradient and updates; Kingma and Ba proposed Adam in 2014, which tunes each parameter's step automatically and is common for training big models.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Each step needs only one backward pass, which is cheap; with a suitable learning rate the error falls step by step.</div>`]},P={n:"Normalisation (LayerNorm)",hl:["an1","an2"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Layer normalization</span> (LayerNorm); the "Add + Norm" blocks in the figure.',"Pulls each character's numbers back to a common scale (mean 0, variance 1) so they do not run off as layers pile up.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Mean μ</span>: the average of the numbers</li><li><span class="k">Variance σ²</span>: how spread out they are around the mean</li><li><span class="k">ε</span>: a tiny number to avoid dividing by 0</li><li><span class="k">γ, β</span>: learned scale and shift</li></ul>',`<ol><li>One character's numbers [1.5, 1.0, 6.0] <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>μ = 2.83, σ² = 5.06, σ = 2.25</li><li><span class="formula">y = (x − μ) ÷ √(σ² + ε)</span></li><li>Gives [−0.59, −0.82, 1.41]: mean 0, variance 1</li><li>Then × γ + β, letting the model choose a suitable scale itself</li></ol><div class="sub">Each character is normalised on its own, independent of other characters and other sentences.</div>`,`<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>As layers stack up, the numbers may grow or shrink more and more, making training unstable and slow.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Ioffe and Szegedy's 2015 BatchNorm found that standardising intermediate results speeds training a lot; but it computes statistics over "a batch of sentences", which does not suit sentences of different lengths or character-by-character generation.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Ba, Kiros and Hinton proposed LayerNorm in 2016: statistics over one character's own numbers only. The original Transformer puts it after the residual addition; from GPT-2 (2019) it usually goes before each block (pre-LN). Some later models use the simpler RMSNorm (Zhang and Sennrich 2019).</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>Every layer receives numbers on the same scale, gradients are smoother, and many layers can be stacked.</div>`]},L={n:"√d scaling",hl:["att"],fr:!1,bk:!1,no:"Lit = blocks that use it",s:['<span class="k">Scaled dot product</span>: attention scores are first divided by √d (d is the size of q and k, written d_k in the paper) before softmax.',"Shrinks dot products back to a normal size so softmax does not give all the attention to one character.",'<ul><li><span class="k">Dot product</span> q·k: a sum of d terms</li><li><span class="k">√d</span>: the square root of the dimension</li><li><span class="k">softmax</span>: turns the scaled scores into attention shares</li></ul>','<ol><li>Random q, k (each number mean 0, variance 1), 100,000 tries each <span class="ex">example numbers</span></li><li>Variance of the dot product: ≈ 4 for d=4, ≈ 16 for d=16, ≈ 64 for d=64; after dividing by √d all ≈ 1</li><li>softmax of scores [8, 4, 0]: 98.17%, 1.8%, 0.03%</li><li>After dividing by √64 = 8, [1, 0.5, 0]: 50.6%, 30.7%, 18.6%</li><li>The X3 cell: each head in this demo has 8 dimensions, so q·k is divided by √8 ≈ 2.83</li></ol>','<div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Problem</span>The bigger d is, the bigger the dot product; softmax is pushed into "one character ≈100%, the rest ≈0", gradients are almost 0 and learning stalls.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Research · finding</span>Vaswani et al. (2017) note that when the parts of q and k are independent with mean 0 and variance 1, q·k has variance d_k; they suspected large dot products put softmax in a region of tiny gradients.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Design</span>Write attention as <span class="formula">softmax(QKᵀ ÷ √d_k) V</span>, just one extra division.</div><div style="margin-top:2px"><span class="t5">Why it works</span>After dividing by √d the variance is back to about 1; whatever d is, the scores stay in a good range, attention spreads properly and gradients behave.</div>']},A={K01:e,K02:a,K03:s,K04:t,K05:n,K06:i,K07:o,K08:l,K09:r,K10:p,K11:d,K12:c,K13:h,K14:m,K15:u,KM01:g,KM02:b,KM03:y,KM04:f,KM05:v,KM06:k,KM07:w,KM08:x,KM09:T,KM10:K,KM11:P,KM12:L};export{e as K01,a as K02,s as K03,t as K04,n as K05,i as K06,o as K07,l as K08,r as K09,p as K10,d as K11,c as K12,h as K13,m as K14,u as K15,g as KM01,b as KM02,y as KM03,f as KM04,v as KM05,k as KM06,w as KM07,x as KM08,T as KM09,K as KM10,P as KM11,L as KM12,A as default};
